SCORE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_FINE_MARGIN | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_FINE_MARGIN | IN_SCOPE | Training-only directed transitions feed successor-residual prediction and scale fine-level hyperbolic geodesic assignment distances; behavior and geometry switches are specified. Numerical safeguards and one-run diagnostics are given.
EVIDENCE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_FINE_MARGIN | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_FINE_MARGIN | Multiplying every item’s code distances by the same positive factor preserves its hard argmin, so the stated behavioral modulation may change soft logits but not exported SID assignments; its benefit depends on soft-training gradients materially changing the learned model.
RATIONALE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_FINE_MARGIN | Behavior 4: measured train entropy/top-1 structure and transition coverage (E5,E10) support the signal, though not this residual-specific SID hypothesis. Geometry 2: the geodesic error is curved, but the per-item positive scalar on all code distances is equivalent to a temperature change in soft assignment and cannot change the hard argmin; the matched Euclidean control is mentioned but no curvature-specific advantage established. Novelty 3: distinct residual prediction computation is described, but prior work is NOT_CHECKED (E15). Downstream 3: altered soft gradients may change fine codes and predictability, but the direct hard assignment effect is absent. Falsifiability 3: switches/diagnostics are present, yet no explicit numerical failure criterion beyond the outcome threshold. Implementability 3: formula is mostly specified; residual coordinates and full geometry guards/cost bound remain risks. Exploration 3: a distinct test of residual prediction, but close to existing successor-geodesic/tangent mechanisms. No Recall prediction is inferred.

SCORE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FRECHET_RESIDUAL | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=2 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FRECHET_RESIDUAL | IN_SCOPE | Training-only branching selects branch-specific Fréchet anchors; curved log/exp residual construction directly feeds fine quantization, and the assignment is expected to change.
EVIDENCE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FRECHET_RESIDUAL | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FRECHET_RESIDUAL | Its specification inconsistently calls the codebook “mapped into the anchor’s tangent space” without defining a common-coordinate decoder/update, while also estimating anchors from changing minibatch residuals; this leaves the actual quantizer and training target ambiguous.
RATIONALE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FRECHET_RESIDUAL | Behavior 4: branching is measured on 21,699 items with mean entropy and degree quantiles (E10), although support is incomplete. Geometry 3: base-point log/exp and Fréchet averaging are genuinely curved, but no matched-control argument rules out a similar Euclidean anchor transform. Novelty 3: branch-specific residual anchors differ computationally from coarse behavior weighting; literature/ledger comparison is not supplied. Downstream 4: branch anchors change fine residual assignment and target branch-conditioned successors. Falsifiability 3: both switches and diagnostic comparisons are specified, but one-run failure criterion is not operationalized. Implementability 2: fixed three iterations and initialization help, but tangent codebook mapping and minibatch-varying anchors need redesign-level clarification. Exploration 3: distinct branch-conditioned mechanism, though closely related to tangent successor residuals. Recall remains unmeasured.

SCORE: TRANSITION_TANGENT_FINE | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=2 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: TRANSITION_TANGENT_FINE | IN_SCOPE | Both provided variants use training-only directed transitions and explicit Poincaré log/exp operations within fine-level residual quantization, with behavior-off and Euclidean switches described. This single block covers the two entries sharing this exact idea_id to avoid duplicate-ID scoring.
EVIDENCE: TRANSITION_TANGENT_FINE | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: TRANSITION_TANGENT_FINE | The specifications are not the same mechanism: one adds a scaled successor tangent vector to residuals using an RMS ratio, while the other uses popularity-adjusted successors and a confidence-scaled exp-map displacement; neither provides evidence that the added direction improves rather than biases assignments.
RATIONALE: TRANSITION_TANGENT_FINE | Behavior 4: measured train transition entropy and coverage substantiate nontrivial successor signals (E5,E10). Geometry 2: maps are explicit, but in a small-radius/tangent regime the operation is approximated by an ordinary vector addition and the geometry-off control could reproduce it; no curvature-specific necessity is shown. Novelty 2: tangent successor steering is a routine variation and the bundle already measures tangent_successor_residual negatively on the recorded diagnostic (E6), though exact proposed variants differ. Downstream 3: moving fine assignment inputs can change codes and prefix predictability, but direction-to-recommendation chain is plausible rather than demonstrated. Falsifiability 3: behavior/geometry/both switches and diagnostics exist, but the two variants need to be separately identified and the one-run falsifier is not concretely bounded. Implementability 2: first variant mixes tangent residual at q with tangent at item h_i without specifying transport; second leaves the precise residual-space relation and bounded coefficient details unclear. Exploration 2: near-repeat of the measured tangent successor residual family. The duplicate proposal distinction is UNKNOWN from the shared idea_id; a bounded check is to inspect the actual candidate records and score each under a unique registry key, without running experiments.

SCORE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_GEODESIC | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=4 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_GEODESIC | IN_SCOPE | Training-only successor-distribution divergence weights a Poincaré geodesic separation objective directly on fine quantized points; behavior-off and Euclidean geometry-off comparisons are stated.
EVIDENCE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_GEODESIC | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: TRANSITION_MARGIN_GEODESIC | The separation loss is nonzero only for pairs closer than a batch-median margin, and its batch pair coverage may be poor; moreover the stated proposal calls for “shared training successors” while the equation compares all minibatch pairs.
RATIONALE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_GEODESIC | Behavior 4: measured train entropy and successor-profile signal support heterogeneous profiles (E5,E9,E10). Geometry 3: geodesic distance directly affects the optimized representation, but Euclidean separation is a plausible matched substitute and curvature necessity is not argued. Novelty 3: pairwise JS-weighted separation differs from steering/assignment bias, but traceable prior-art comparison is absent. Downstream 4: fine points/codebooks can separate items with divergent futures, with SID and prefix diagnostics named. Falsifiability 3: three modes and a target are stated, but explicit failure threshold/diagnostic discriminator is limited. Implementability 4: distributions, JS, median scale and pair cost are concrete; numerical safeguards are not fully specified. Exploration 3: distinct pair-separation mechanism, though within the existing successor-margin family. One run cannot isolate switches, as policy requires.

SCORE: TRANSITION_CURVATURE_MARGIN | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: TRANSITION_CURVATURE_MARGIN | IN_SCOPE | Popularity-adjusted training successor profiles weight a fine-residual Poincaré margin, coupling behavior and curved distance in the same objective, with the required switches described in the bundle.
EVIDENCE: TRANSITION_CURVATURE_MARGIN | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: TRANSITION_CURVATURE_MARGIN | No pairs are actually specified for the minibatch term beyond a generic sampled pair set, and the derived initialization margin may be miscalibrated as residuals evolve; this makes effective behavior coverage and gradient scale uncertain.
RATIONALE: TRANSITION_CURVATURE_MARGIN | Behavior 4: train-only successor statistics are plausible and measured (E5,E10). Geometry 2: the hyperbolic distance is explicit, but Euclidean distance with the same pair weights/margin could implement the same separation objective, and no cancellation/necessity analysis is given. Novelty 2: routine successor-weighted pairwise margin, closely overlapping TRANSITION_MARGIN_GEODESIC. Downstream 3: fine residual separation could alter assignments, but no specific successor-token consequence is supported. Falsifiability 3: modes and outcome are stated, though pairs/criteria are not fully matched or discriminating. Implementability 3: equation and fixed margin rule are given, but pair sampling, safeguards and aggregation remain underspecified. Exploration 2: small variation on measured successor-geodesic coarse and margin family. Literature prior art is UNKNOWN; a bounded literature/ledger check would resolve novelty grounding.

SCORE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_RESIDUAL | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=1 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_RESIDUAL | OUT_OF_SCOPE | Although behavior and curved operators are named, the behavior-dependent alpha_i multiplies only a loss comparing a code-to-residual log vector with a behavior barycenter vector; when alpha_i is removed the core hard quantization is unchanged, and the stated “pull reconstruction toward” mechanism does not follow clearly from that equation. Its behavior-off/geometry-off controls do not resolve the coupling ambiguity.
EVIDENCE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_RESIDUAL | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_RESIDUAL | The alleged behavior-to-assignment pathway is not in the argmin: behavior enters only an auxiliary term after k_i is chosen, so this is an unchanged quantizer with a separate behavior loss, contrary to the scope contract.
RATIONALE: TRANSITION_GEODESIC_RESIDUAL | Behavior 4: directed transition support and entropy are measured (E5,E10). Geometry 2: log/exp and distance are explicit but curvature’s effect may be reproduced by a Euclidean tangent objective; no matched-control necessity argument. Novelty 2: close to existing successor barycenter/tangent residual work. Downstream 3: the auxiliary gradient could change representations over training, but does not directly make the stated assignment behavior-conditioned. Falsifiability 3: switches and diagnostics are named, yet they do not establish the required connected pathway. Implementability 1: alpha and sparse graph are described, but the displayed loss’s points/tangent spaces and b_i dimensions are inconsistent or ambiguous (r_i in tangent, b_i in ball). Exploration 2: a variant of successor residual objectives. UNKNOWN dimension detail: bounded check is a tensor-shape/unit audit of every Log/Exp argument and the actual computational graph, not another training run.

SCORE: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=4 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | IN_SCOPE | Training-only successor-category entropy determines a radial displacement of curved code prototypes inside final fine assignment; switches and diagnostics are specified.
EVIDENCE: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | The radial displacement is parallel to each codeword, so it changes prototype norm rather than adding branch-specific angular structure; equal-norm codewords receive the same radial offset and the mechanism can collapse to an item-dependent scale/radius bias.
RATIONALE: BRANCH_RADIAL_FINE_CODES | Behavior 4: measured entropy stratification supports branching, though category-level entropy coverage for this exact statistic is not reported (E5,E10). Geometry 2: Poincaré distance is explicit, but a matched Euclidean displaced-prototype rule could reproduce the behavior and curvature value is not established. Novelty 2: radial prototype displacement is a small variation of behavior-conditioned fine assignment. Downstream 4: entropy-dependent prototypes can change final codes and branch-conditioned predictability. Falsifiability 3: behavior/geometry controls and observable assignments are identified, but no explicit discriminator or failure threshold. Implementability 4: formulas, scales, preprocessing and cost are specified; safeguards around zero codes/ball clipping are only partly covered. Exploration 2: near relative to other conditioned-prototype mechanisms. Literature/ledger status UNKNOWN; bounded check is computational comparison with the cited proposal ledger.

SCORE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | IN_SCOPE | Directed training transitions define a differentiable pairwise ranking term using Poincaré distances and the source’s final fine codeword; the bundle specifies behavior-off and Euclidean controls.
EVIDENCE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E7 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | With the stated score s=d(source, assigned code)^2-d(successor, same source code)^2, minimizing softplus(s) pulls the successor toward the source code; it does not establish that successor distinctions are preserved and may actively merge them.
RATIONALE: TRANSITION_MARGIN_FINE | Behavior 4: train transitions and successor statistics have measured support (E5,E10). Geometry 2: curved distances are explicit but a Euclidean ranking loss can match the same ordering, with no argument that curvature is essential. Novelty 2: ordinary transition ranking on quantization scores is a small variant of existing successor objectives. Downstream 3: gradients reach fine codebooks and residuals, but the hypothesized distinction preservation conflicts with the loss direction. Falsifiability 3: switches, diagnostics and frozen test criterion are named; no separate one-run criterion for the mechanism beyond Recall. Implementability 3: edge sampling and gradients are implementable, though coefficient 1 is not derived from loss-scale evidence and geometry safeguards are absent. Exploration 2: near-repeat of transition ranking/margin mechanisms. UNKNOWN prior-art grounding: a bounded cited-work and internal-ledger lookup would resolve novelty, without an experiment.

SCORE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=2 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | IN_SCOPE | Training-only per-item successor covariance transforms the fine residual before Poincaré assignment; the curved assignment and behavior-derived frame form one pathway.
EVIDENCE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | The stated shrinkage formula uses alpha_i=n_i/(n_i+D) both as a weight toward global covariance and as an additive global-variance term, but the resulting covariance scaling is not clearly a convex shrinkage; diagonal inverse-square-root transforms may also make the shared-codeword decode inconsistent.
RATIONALE: BRANCH_CONDITIONED_FINE_BASIS | Behavior 4: train-only successor branching has measured entropy/degree coverage (E5,E10), but covariance-specific support is not measured. Geometry 2: Poincaré mapping and distance are present, yet behavior enters through a linear whitening transform that a matched Euclidean quantizer can apply identically; geometry appears largely separable. Novelty 3: item-specific successor covariance frames are computationally distinct from successor mean steering, although cited work/ledger comparison is absent. Downstream 3: transforms can alter final assignments and preserve high-variance directions, but no directional recommendation chain is validated. Falsifiability 3: switches and diagnostics are included, with no explicit falsifying diagnostic beyond recall. Implementability 2: diagonal approximation is affordable, but formula, inverse transform, covariance units and decoder consistency need clarification. Exploration 3: a distinct covariance-based failure mode; whether it helps is UNKNOWN, resolvable by a bounded shape/conditioning audit and the single prescribed run, not sweeps.
